<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamaj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Hamaj Regency</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hamaj were a people of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> whose leaders ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj Sultanate of Sennar</a> as hereditary regents from 1761. Their founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abu-likaylik">Muhammad Abu Likaylik</a>, deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badi-iv">Badi IV</a>, and the Hamaj regents kept the Funj sultans as figureheads while struggling among themselves. Their regime ended when the Egyptian army of Muhammad Ali conquered Sennar in 1821.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Hamaj descended from people of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, known as the Shankalla, who lived there before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> arrived and whose language belongs to the Koman branch. They were often targets of Funj slave raids, and hamaj, meaning riffraff, was a contemptuous name the Funj gave them. Funj society was strictly divided by skin colour: the Funj called themselves blue and the Hamaj red. Incorporated into the Funj Sultanate of Sennar in the 17th century, the Hamaj prospered, and according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-chronicle">Funj Chronicle</a> the family of a slave called Hamaj grew until it held power and prestige in the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badi-iv">Badi IV</a> angered the old Funj families by giving their lands to new non-Funj followers and by relying on slave armies. He made the Hamaj general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abu-likaylik">Muhammad Abu Likaylik</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> in 1747. The Funj armies there agreed to depose the sultan under Abu Likaylik's leadership, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>, were joined by Badi's son Nasir and surrounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a>. Badi was allowed to leave under an amnesty, and Abu Likaylik installed Nasir as sultan, beginning the regency.</p><p>Its sixty years were marked by constant strife between regents and sultans and within Abu Likaylik's family. Eight years after his accession Nasir was deposed, and he was executed when found conspiring to return. Regent followed regent, often by murder and revenge. The Funj nearly overthrew the Hamaj in 1785, when Sultan Adlan II seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a> and the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a> was killed in November at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a>, but the Hamaj later defeated Adlan and restored their regency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> was lost to the Keira Sultanate of Darfur in 1787, and by 1821 sultans and regents controlled little beyond the city of Sennar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>As the Egyptian army of Ismail Pasha advanced in 1821, the regent Muhammad wad Adlan was overthrown and killed by his cousin Hasan wad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a>. Hasan fled towards the Ethiopian border, and the last Funj sultan, Badi VII, submitted in person, probably on 12 June. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a> unopposed the next day and found the royal palace derelict. The Hamaj chieftain Idris Muhammad Adlan held out in the Funj mountains until 1826 and served there as shaykh until 1851. After that the Hamaj clans had little influence in Sudanese public life.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hamaj Regency” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As late as 1930, <b>Hamaj</b> villagers in the southern Gezira would swear by 'Soba the home of my grandfathers and grandmothers which can make the stone float and the cotton ball sink'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After Badi IV (r. 1724–1762) was deposed in 1762 by Muhammad Abu Likaylik, leader of the <b>Hamaj</b>, a people of the southern Gezira, Hamaj regents governed in the sultans’ name and installed or removed sultans at will until the end of the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abdallab lost much of their power in the 18th century as the <b>Hamaj</b> regents dominated Sennar.</blockquote><a href="/state/abdallab-sheikhdom">Abdallab Sheikhdom</a></li></ul></section>
